## Every execve inside a human login session, tagged so that vigil reads its own records.
##
##   auid>=1000 auid!=unset   only what a person started. Without them every exec of every
##                            service enters the stream. What daemons and service accounts run
##                            is not seen here; vigil's process collector reads that.
##   arch=b64 and arch=b32    both: a 32-bit process walks past a b64-only rule. b32 is second
##                            because a kernel without 32-bit support refuses it and augenrules
##                            reports the refusal. Keep the order.
##   -k vigil_exec            the tag vigil selects its own records by. Your rules are untouched.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k vigil_exec
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve -F auid>=1000 -F auid!=unset -k vigil_exec
